A||N||17||Dave85||Suspension ??||||
Z||000000||Dave85||09-08-1999||06:15 PM||||<BR>I am wanting to upgrade the front suspension and was wondering where the best place is to get bushings,  ball joints and springs. Is this a very hard job to do? I'm not overly mechanically inclined, but I'm willing to learn.  Thanks!||||reg||
Z||000001||mshill||09-08-1999||06:33 PM||||Check out <A HREF="http://www.suspension.com" TARGET=_blank>www.suspension.com</A> <P>I just bought urethane bushings for front and rear A-arms, front swaybar and endlinks, engine cradle, and dogbone.<P>They have a good page on removing/installing urethane bushings.  It does not sound easy, but doesn't sound like rocket science either.<P>Mark S.||||reg||
Z||000002||theogre||09-08-1999||08:48 PM||||time consuming, not dificult. ||||reg||
Z||000003||Dave85||09-09-1999||10:04 AM||||I went to that site and didn't find too much.  The stuff that they do have, it's cheaper at the Fiero Store. Has anyboby used the F.S. springs?  And what about ball joints? Thanks  ||||reg||
Z||000004||theogre||09-09-1999||02:25 PM||||suspension.com is bushings only.......<P>shop local autoparts stored for the rest first. Don't even consider changing the springs or sway until after you rebush everything. The urethane will change things allot all by itself. If you order springs based on the current configuration you'll probably hate yourself.<P>Use Moog ball joints.||||reg||
Z||000005||PaUL||09-09-1999||03:58 PM||||For balljoints forget FS. The ones they have are std. Moog parts available at any parts store. The "adjustable" top balljoint they sell are also std Moog parts. BTW the T car (chevette) uses the same front balljoints and the X and A cars use the same rears.||||reg||
Z||000006||lowCG||09-09-1999||04:49 PM||||The moog balljoints are the way to go,the others often suffer from poor manufacturing practices,and fail to last more than a couple of months.As for bushings in the front,you should seriously consider what you're going to be using the car for;the rear is where the response is going be more rewarding,and the ride will suffer more from the poly in the front versus the rear as well.For anything but track use,I'd stick with the rubber in the front,most of the time only the upper bushings are showing wear.I'd spend the $ on the shocks,however,Koni,or Bilstein,if you can afford it.<BR>As for the springs,these can be pretty hazardous to work with unless you have some experience working with large metal objects,and have a pretty decent spring compressor.The cheap ones are scary,used one once about 15 years ago,can't see how they can even sell those things.||||reg||
Z||000007||Ozzy||09-09-1999||08:31 PM||||My coupla pescos worth...<P>Ditto on da moogs! Just put a set in the lowers. Gotta hunt for a good price though. I got mine thru a rep for $47.00 each. <P>As for the bushings, I bought mine from the guys at  <A HREF="http://www.suspension.com" TARGET=_blank>www.suspension.com</A> . The owner was very helpfull and he sells the full front Prothane replacement set of bushings for about $20.00 less than FS. ||||reg||
Z||000008||Dave85||09-09-1999||09:52 PM||||Thanks all. I really don't care about the ride quality. I'm interested in performance. I want to be able to race this car someday.||||reg||
Z||000009||theogre||09-09-1999||10:27 PM||||Doing it one phase at a time is just as important for racing. If you shot gun it you won't know where you hosed it when you don't like the results. I can about guarantee you won't like the result....... ||||reg||
Z||000010||lowCG||09-09-1999||10:55 PM||||Precisely.<BR>The original front,lower bushings are two different durometer's;soft on the leading side,firm in back.<BR>Why would the factory(same one that screenprinted the markings on the dash)bother and have two different part numbers if there wasn't a functional reason,like adhesion/control?<P> ||||reg||
Z||000011||Dave85||09-09-1999||11:08 PM||||So, should I stay away from Poly then? I need to replace them and simply want the BEST handling I can get. My car has 113,000 miles and all original parts. Already have done the swaybar with Poly because one of the end links was broken.  Thanks||||reg||
Z||000012||Standard||09-10-1999||03:29 AM||||Are broken endlinks a common thing on the Fiero?  I broke an endlink a while ago. Annoying, but now I have an excuse to go to poly bushings if I ever get around to finding some||||reg||
Z||000013||batboy||09-11-1999||11:31 AM||||Dave, I wouldn't stay away from the poly bushings if you are after performance and a stiffer suspension. You have to weigh the pros and cons. I've got almost all my parts collected for an entire rebuild of front and rear control arm bushing, ball joints, and shocks. Plus new front springs, sway bar bushings, sway bar end links, and tie rod ends. I'm hoping my sloppy old 85 will handle better than a new 88 when I'm done.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Dave85||09-11-1999||12:09 PM||||Thanks batboy!  My car is a daily driver but it only sees about 20 miles per day. The roads are pretty good here and I don't mind a firm ride. I do however want the best performance I can get!<BR>Dave||||reg||6
Z||000015||batboy||09-11-1999||03:13 PM||||Dave, since you mentioned early on that you are not a certified mechanic, you may want to consider doing your suspension mods a step at a time, like was stated in an earlier post. Tires are a great place to start and are real easy to swap around. Lots of handling performance can be gained in a good set of tires. Shocks and struts are another often overlooked suspension upgrade. Koni and Bilstein are the best, but premium Monroe stuff is pretty good for less than half the price. My experience with Fieros has shown that the front lower ball joints and the inner tie rod ends wear out fairly often. Sloppiness in either of these parts will cause poor handling. Replace anything in the suspension with wear on it first. Then think about installing a rear sway bar. Good luck!||||reg||1
Z||000016||Dave85||09-11-1999||10:43 PM||||Thanks again! I'm definately in need of shocks and struts. Tires are almost new(well the back ones are a little worn). I was planning on purchasing everything as I can, and then do it all at once. Has anyone found the best prices on shocks/struts?||||reg||1
Z||000017||batboy||09-12-1999||11:07 AM||||That's a very ambious job to do all at once if you're never done much suspension work before. Chassis bolts tend to be rusted solid so a torch and impact tools are handy to have.<P>You'll need a heavy duty hydraulic floor jack or two, jack stands, big blocks of wood, a big pry bar, grease gun, and the normal heavy duty mechanic tools such as wrenches and sockets.<P>In addition, you need to buy or rent the following special tools: rear strut spring compressor, front coil spring compressor, ball joint press, pickle fork, and inner tie rod end tool.<P>After all the work is done, then you must take the car to a shop and have a 4 wheel alignment done. This suspension work is not easy or cheap. Again, I suggest just doing a little bit at a time, instead of biting off way more than you can chew. Good luck.||||reg||1
